# Generated SQL migrations land here and are committed.
#
# Two kinds live side by side:
#   - files drizzle-kit generates from src/schema
#   - hand-written SQL for things Drizzle does not model: the movement
#     immutability trigger, the derived views, and the REVOKE on the app role
#
# This folder, not the TypeScript, is the real source of truth for the
# database. Never run db:push against anything holding real stock — it
# resolves constraint drift by offering to truncate.
#
# ── meta/ and the baseline snapshot ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# drizzle-kit does not read the .sql files to work out what the database
# looks like. It reads meta/, takes the last snapshot by filename, and diffs
# src/schema against that. So the snapshot — not the migration count — is what
# makes db:generate correct.
#
# Snapshots 0000–0003 are the ones drizzle-kit wrote when those migrations
# were generated. 0004–0031 were hand-written, so no snapshot was ever
# recorded for them, and the baseline sat at 0003 while the database moved on
# by twenty-eight migrations. db:generate then offered to re-create `batch`,
# `design_descriptor` and every column added since — and, having no snapshot
# to work from, asked which of them were renames, which is what made it
# demand a TTY.
#
# 0032_snapshot.json closes that gap. It is the serialisation of src/schema
# itself, checked against a drizzle-kit pull of the real database first: all
# 147 columns across the 12 tables agreed, and nothing declared in TypeScript
# was missing from the database.
#
# It is deliberately built from the TypeScript rather than from the pull. The
# database also carries eight CHECK constraints, two indexes, the
# colourway_on_hand view and the movement trigger, none of which Drizzle's
# schema DSL can express. A snapshot introspected from the database would
# record them, then diff them against a TypeScript schema that cannot mention
# them, and the next db:generate would offer to drop the lot.
#
# The pull also found nineteen constraints created by hand-written SQL and so
# named by Postgres (design_blouse_border_id_fkey) rather than by Drizzle
# (design_blouse_border_id_lookup_value_id_fk) — same columns, same behaviour,
# a name the snapshot did not use. 0033 renames them to match. What the
# database still holds that no snapshot can is the part Drizzle's schema DSL
# cannot express: eight CHECK constraints, two indexes, the colourway_on_hand
# view and the movement trigger. Those live in hand-written SQL and nowhere
# else, which is the other half of why this folder is the source of truth.
#
# So the invariant to check, if db:generate ever starts offering strange
# things again: the highest-numbered snapshot should match the
# highest-numbered migration. 0003 against 0032 was what broke it.
#
# ── meta/_journal.json and the order things run in ──────────────────────────
#
# The second invariant, and the one that fails quietly: `when` must increase
# with `idx`.
#
# drizzle decides what to apply by comparing timestamps, not by remembering
# what it has run:
#
#     if (!lastDbMigration || Number(lastDbMigration.created_at) < migration.folderMillis)
#
# — drizzle-orm/pg-core/dialect.js. A migration runs only when its journal
# `when` is greater than the highest already recorded. The hash is stored and
# never compared. So one entry out of order and it is skipped: not applied,
# not recorded, no error, and the next deploy does not retry it.
#
# That has happened twice. Both times a `when` was written by hand and put in
# the future, and the next generated migration got the real clock, which was
# smaller. 0038 added a column that then was not there.
#
# `pnpm db:check` refuses if the journal is out of order, names a file the
# folder does not have, or has a file the journal does not name — the three
# ways this folder has gone wrong so far. It runs ahead of `db:migrate`, so a
# bad journal stops the deploy in the build log rather than in production a
# day later. `pnpm db:check --fix` repairs the ordering, and `db:generate`
# runs it automatically, so the trap is not laid in the first place.
#
# Do not hand-write a `when`. Let db:generate and --fix decide it.
